# edgeconnect-mcp settings
#
# Copy this file to .env, fill in your values and keep it private:
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
# Never commit .env. It holds your Orchestrator API keys.
#
# Format rules:
#   - One KEY=value per line. Lines starting with # are ignored.
#   - Put comments on their own line. Text after a value on the same line becomes part of the value.
#   - Quotes are optional: key="value" and key=value mean the same.
#   - Environment variables with the same name override this file (handy with docker --env-file).
#
# Point the server at this file with --dotenv /absolute/path/.env or EDGECONNECT_DOTENV.
# Without either, it reads ./.env from the folder it is started in.


# =====================================================================
# 1. Orchestrators (required)
# =====================================================================
#
# Give each Orchestrator a numbered slot and a nickname: orch_1, orch_2, ...
# The nickname is what you and the assistant use ("show the appliances on lab").
# It may use lowercase letters, digits and _, and it prefixes all of that
# Orchestrator's settings below. Reserved names: default, orchestrator, edgeconnect, ec.
#
# Each Orchestrator needs at least:
#   <nickname>_base_url  https:// address of the Orchestrator. Host only, or host + /gms/rest.
#                        Plain http:// is refused.
#   <nickname>_api_key   a REST API key, created on the Orchestrator with the least privilege
#                        you need (read-only is enough for reads). How to create one:
#                        https://arubanetworking.hpe.com/techdocs/sdwan/docs/orch/orchestrator/server/api-key/

orch_1=lab
lab_base_url=https://<ORCHESTRATOR_FQDN_OR_IP>
lab_api_key=<API_KEY>

# A second Orchestrator, for example production, kept read-only:
# orch_2=prod
# prod_base_url=https://orchestrator.example.com
# prod_api_key=<API_KEY>

# Which Orchestrator a tool call uses when it names none. A nickname or a slot.
# Without it, the lowest-numbered slot (usually orch_1) is the default.
# orch_default=lab
# orch_default=orch_2


# =====================================================================
# 2. TLS: trusting the Orchestrator's certificate
# =====================================================================
#
# Certificates are always verified. If your Orchestrator has a certificate
# from a public CA, you need nothing here. Otherwise pick ONE of these.
#
# a) Private CA: point at the CA certificate (PEM) that signed the Orchestrator's certificate.
# lab_ca_bundle=/etc/ssl/certs/my-company-root-ca.pem
#
# b) Self-signed certificate: pin its SHA-256 fingerprint (64 hex digits; colons are fine).
#    Get it with the command below, and compare it with what the Orchestrator console
#    shows, so you know you are pinning the real server:
#      openssl s_client -connect <ORCHESTRATOR>:443 </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256
#    After a certificate renewal the pin no longer matches, and calls fail until you update it.
# lab_tls_sha256=AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89


# =====================================================================
# 3. What the assistant may change (per Orchestrator)
# =====================================================================
#
# Every Orchestrator is READ-ONLY until you opt in. Accepted "on" values: true, yes, 1.
#
# Allow api_write (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) on this Orchestrator. Turn it on for
# lab Orchestrators, or deliberately; leave production read-only.
# lab_mcp_writes=true
#
# Also allow writes through the raw passthroughs /appliance/rest and /broadcastCli.
# These forward any request to an appliance's own API or CLI, so the spec can't limit
# what they change. Needs _mcp_writes=true as well.
# lab_mcp_passthrough=true
#
# Two-step writes, ON by default. The first api_write call sends nothing: it returns the
# exact change and a one-time confirmation code. The assistant shows you the change and
# only after your yes repeats the identical call with the code. A code is good for one
# use, 10 minutes, the identical request and the client it was issued to.
# Turn it off only for unattended automation you trust. Accepted "off" values: false, no, 0, off.
# lab_mcp_confirm_writes=false
#
# High-risk writes stay refused even with writes on, unless you name their category.
# Comma-separated, or "all". Categories:
#   auth, certificates, licensing, backup_restore, upgrade, reboot, orchestrator_ha,
#   appliance_lifecycle, cloud_deployment, provisioning, orchestrator_admin,
#   identity_events, cli, ip_allowlist, traffic_disruptive
# See exactly what is refused with:  edgeconnect-mcp --dotenv .env --list-high-risk lab
# lab_mcp_allow_high_risk=reboot,upgrade
# lab_mcp_allow_high_risk=all


# =====================================================================
# 4. Rate limits and API spec (per Orchestrator, optional)
# =====================================================================
#
# Requests per second. Over the limit, calls wait instead of failing.
# Bursts of twice the rate are allowed. 0 = no limit. Defaults: reads 10, writes 2.
# lab_mcp_read_rate=10
# lab_mcp_write_rate=2
#
# The server downloads the API spec from the Orchestrator (/gms/rest/apiDocs) on first use.
# To use a saved copy instead (offline, or to pin a version), give one JSON file
# or a folder of *.json files:
# lab_openapi=/home/me/edgeconnect/lab-apiDocs.json
# lab_openapi=/home/me/edgeconnect/specs/
#
# Header the API key is sent in. Only change it if your Orchestrator expects another one.
# lab_api_key_header=X-Auth-Token


# =====================================================================
# 5. Audit log
# =====================================================================
#
# Every tool call is recorded: UTC timestamp, weekday, session, client address, tool,
# Orchestrator, method, path, what the operation does (its API summary), for writes the
# step (awaiting_confirmation, confirmed or executed) and the request body, the query,
# outcome, error, duration and response size. Secrets never reach the log: bodies hold
# <REDACTED:...> tokens, and any secret typed in clear under a secret-named key is masked.
#
# JSON-lines file, on by default, mode 600. Set off (or none, false, 0) to disable.
# mcp_audit_log=~/.edgeconnect-mcp/audit.jsonl
# mcp_audit_log=off
#
# Also send each record to a syslog server (optional). Plain syslog is not encrypted,
# so prefer the local syslog daemon (rsyslog, syslog-ng) and let it forward over TLS:
#   Linux local daemon:   mcp_audit_syslog=/dev/log
#   macOS local daemon:   mcp_audit_syslog=/var/run/syslog
#   UDP on a trusted net: mcp_audit_syslog=udp://10.1.2.20:514
#   TCP on a trusted net: mcp_audit_syslog=tcp://10.1.2.20:514
# Records over 8 KB go to syslog without the body (the file and SQLite keep it).
# Syslog is a copy, not the record of truth: records sent while the server is down are
# missing there (stderr says so). Over TCP the next record after it returns reconnects.
# The file and SQLite keep every record.
# mcp_audit_syslog=/dev/log
#
# Also write each record to a SQLite database (optional), for queries such as
#   sqlite3 ~/.edgeconnect-mcp/audit.db "SELECT ts, client, method, path, phase, outcome FROM audit WHERE method != 'GET'"
# mcp_audit_sqlite=~/.edgeconnect-mcp/audit.db


# =====================================================================
# 6. HTTP mode (only with --http PORT; stdio needs none of this)
# =====================================================================
#
# HTTP mode is for clients that need a URL. Put an HTTPS reverse proxy (Caddy,
# Nginx Proxy Manager) in front of it; see docs/remote-access.md.
# With HTTP mode on, the server refuses to start if this file is readable by
# other users (chmod 600 .env).
#
# Access token, required. At least 32 characters. Generate one with:
#   python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))'
# Clients send it as:  Authorization: Bearer <token>
# mcp_http_token=<PASTE_A_LONG_RANDOM_TOKEN>
#
# Optional second token that can read but can never call api_write.
# Give this one to people or tools that only need to look. It must differ from mcp_http_token.
# mcp_http_read_token=<PASTE_ANOTHER_LONG_RANDOM_TOKEN>
#
# Networks allowed to connect: IP addresses or subnets, comma-separated.
# Default: this machine only (127.0.0.1,::1).
# A refused client gets an error naming the address the server saw, so you know what to add.
# Examples:
#   your laptop's VPN range:       mcp_http_allowed_clients=10.8.0.0/24
#   two office subnets:            mcp_http_allowed_clients=10.1.2.0/24,192.168.50.0/24
#   one admin workstation:         mcp_http_allowed_clients=10.1.2.15
# mcp_http_allowed_clients=127.0.0.1,::1
#
# Proxies whose X-Forwarded-For header is believed. When a request comes from one of
# these, the client is the last address the proxy added. From anyone else the header
# is ignored, so a client can't pretend to be an allowed address.
# Default: this machine only, which fits a proxy on the same machine (Caddy, nginx).
# Example, Nginx Proxy Manager in Docker on a network with subnet 172.30.0.0/24:
#   mcp_http_trusted_proxies=172.30.0.0/24
# mcp_http_trusted_proxies=127.0.0.1,::1
#
# Address the server listens on. Default 127.0.0.1, so plain HTTP never leaves the machine.
# The container image sets 0.0.0.0 (inside the container only); compose.yaml then publishes
# the port on the host's 127.0.0.1. You should not need to change this outside a container.
# mcp_http_listen=127.0.0.1
#
# Browser origins allowed to call the server, comma-separated. Default: none, so web
# pages open in a browser can't call it. Only set this for a browser-based MCP client.
# mcp_http_allowed_origins=https://my-mcp-client.example.com
#
# Putting it together:
#
#   Server on this machine, Caddy in front, clients on the home LAN 192.168.1.0/24:
#     mcp_http_token=<token>
#     mcp_http_allowed_clients=192.168.1.0/24
#     (Caddy connects from 127.0.0.1, which the default trusted proxies already cover.)
#
#   Server container behind Nginx Proxy Manager on Docker network 172.30.0.0/24,
#   clients on the VPN 10.8.0.0/24:
#     mcp_http_token=<token>
#     mcp_http_trusted_proxies=172.30.0.0/24
#     mcp_http_allowed_clients=10.8.0.0/24
